- wasm-node-glue-fetch-crash: qjsm.wasm's Emscripten-generated glue
  (build/wasm32-unknown-emscripten/qjsm.js, emitted by emsdk's bundled
  emscripten 2.0.10) picks the wasm-loading path in `instantiateAsync()`
  by checking only `typeof fetch === 'function'`, with no
  `ENVIRONMENT_IS_NODE` guard (qjsm.js:1534-1554). Node 18+ ships a global
  `fetch`, so under any modern Node this branch runs `fetch(wasmBinaryFile,
  ...)` with a plain filesystem path instead of the Node-native
  `readFileSync` path a few lines below, and `fetch()` throws immediately
  because the argument isn't a URL. This is a known compatibility gap
  between old Emscripten releases and Node's newer global fetch, fixed in
  more recent Emscripten by adding the environment check; upgrading emsdk
  (or patching the generated glue) would fix it properly. Found while
  smoke-testing qjsm.wasm after enabling QUICKJS_INTERNAL for the wasm
  build. Workaround below unblocks manual testing but isn't a real fix.

    node -e "delete globalThis.fetch;
             process.argv=[process.argv[0],'qjsm.js','-e','console.log(1)'];
             require('./qjsm.js')"
    // without the `delete globalThis.fetch`: TypeError: Failed to parse
    // URL from qjsm.wasm
    // with it: prints 1 as expected

- wasm-qjsm-no-real-file-imports: qjsm.wasm has no filesystem wired up to
  the host when run under Node (`-sNODERAWFS` was never set, see
  ../CMakeLists.txt:43-68, and no `--preload-file`/NODEFS mount is done
  either) -- only `MEMFS`, which starts empty. Every native/JS builtin
  module registered via BUILTINS_NATIVE/BUILTINS_COMPILED loads fine
  (`--list` shows all 27 native + 14 compiled-JS modules, and `-e` can
  import and use them), but `-m <realfile.js>` for any file that actually
  lives on disk fails because that file was never copied into MEMFS. This
  means qjsm.wasm today can only run inline `-e` scripts (or scripts naming
  a builtin module by name) under Node, not arbitrary local .js files --
  the primary way qjs-modules' own lib/*.js wrappers are normally loaded.
  Found in the same wasm smoke-test session as the entry above.

    // From build/wasm32-unknown-emscripten, with a file test.js sitting
    // right next to qjsm.js/qjsm.wasm:
    node -e "delete globalThis.fetch;
             process.argv=[process.argv[0],'qjsm.js','-m','test.js'];
             require('./qjsm.js')"
    // expected: test.js runs
    // actual: ReferenceError: could not load module 'test.js': ReferenceError:
    //         could not load module filename 'test.js'

- js-module-loader-detection-vs-actual-headers (CMakeLists.txt:544, cmake/QuickJSModule.cmake:4):
  the configure-time probe that decides whether qjsm.c should call the 3-arg
  or 4-arg js_module_loader() compiles its test snippet against
  ${QUICKJS_SOURCES_ROOT} (the sibling ../quickjs checkout, which this
  monorepo edits/rebuilds continuously) via compile_code()'s
  "-DINCLUDE_DIRECTORIES=${QUICKJS_SOURCES_ROOT}", but the real qjsm.c
  translation units are compiled with "-I/usr/local/include/quickjs" (the
  separately installed, not-necessarily-in-sync system headers). when the
  sibling source's js_module_loader() signature has been edited but
  /usr/local hasn't been reinstalled yet, the probe result no longer
  matches what qjsm.c actually sees, so JS_MODULE_LOADER_OLD gets set
  wrong for the real build - this can either fail the build outright
  ("too many arguments to function js_module_loader") or, worse, compile
  cleanly with a mismatched signature. probes trust the live sibling source tree while actual
  compilation/linking trusts the separately installed /usr/local quickjs,
  and nothing checks the two agree.

    // rm -rf build/x86_64-linux-gnu && cmake -B build/x86_64-linux-gnu -S .
    // && cmake --build build/x86_64-linux-gnu --target qjsm
    // actual (intermittent, depends on how ../quickjs/quickjs-libc.h
    // currently reads vs. what's installed under /usr/local/include/quickjs):
    //   error: too many arguments to function 'js_module_loader'
    // expected: consistent, correct macro selection every configure

- bnf-lexer-literal-newline-in-l-file (lib/lexer/bnf.js): BNFLexer throws
  "No matching token" on tests/Shell-Grammar.l's definitions section
  because a literal newline character inside a flex character-class
  substitution (the `NL  \n` definition line) doesn't match any DIRECTIVE
  or top-level rule - the escape sequence `\n` there is meant as the
  regex metachar, but nothing in bnf.js's DIRECTIVE state rules matches a
  bare `\` followed by `n` outside of `d_string`. Found while running the
  new tests/test_lexer.js highlighter over tests/Shell-Grammar.l.

    import BNFLexer from './lib/lexer/bnf.js';
    import { readFileSync } from 'fs';
    const lex = new BNFLexer(readFileSync('tests/Shell-Grammar.l', 'utf-8'), 'tests/Shell-Grammar.l');
    for (const tok of lex) {}
    // actual: throws InternalError: ...:4:5: No matching token (0: INITIAL)
    // expected: lexes the whole file without throwing

- pointer-atom-index-ignores-leading-zero (include/utils.h:753,
  js_atom_is_index): treats any atom whose string form parses as a
  non-negative decimal (e.g. "01") as an array index, so
  Pointer#toString()/toRFC6901()/atoms_serialize() render it as an
  index (`[1]` / `/1`) instead of the literal string key ("01"),
  losing the leading zero. ECMAScript's array-index canonical form (and
  RFC 6901's array-index grammar) both require no leading zero except
  for "0" itself, so "01" should stay a plain string key. Shared by
  every module that reads array-index-ness off an atom via
  js_atom_is_index, not pointer-specific. Found while adding RFC 6901
  support to quickjs-pointer.c/src/pointer.c.

    import { Pointer } from 'pointer';
    Pointer.from(["01", "x"]).toString();
    // "[1].x" -- should be ".01.x" (leading zero silently dropped)

- excluded-native-modules-unreachable-without-shared-build (CMakeLists.txt):
  BUILD_SHARED_MODULES defaults OFF, so a from-scratch build never produces
  the .so files that BUILTINS_NATIVE_EXCLUDE-excluded native modules
  (lexer, location, repeater, tree-walker, child-process) or a module
  dropped from BUILTINS_COMPILED (e.g. stack) would need for qjsm's
  runtime dynamic-load fallback -- import of those names fails outright.
  test_lexer.js/test_location.js/test_repeater.js/test_treewalker.js/
  test_childprocess.js/test_stack.js all pass in build/x86_64-linux-gnu
  today only because that directory still has stale .so files left over
  from an earlier build configuration (mtimes predate the current
  CMakeLists.txt logic) -- they are not regenerated by a current build,
  and a genuinely fresh build directory (e.g. build/x86_64-linux-bundle)
  fails all six tests. Found while setting up the bundle build.

    cmake -B build/fresh -S . && cmake --build build/fresh
    ctest --test-dir build/fresh -R test_lexer.js  # fails: could not load module 'lexer'

- pigpio-submodule-unused (CMakeLists.txt:26,389-398,742-744;
  quickjs-gpio.c): the `pigpio` git submodule is vendored
  (.gitmodules) and README/Building's gating table implies `gpio`
  needs an external GPIO library, but `quickjs-gpio.c` never includes
  any pigpio header (it does direct memory-mapped register access
  instead), and every CMakeLists.txt reference to pigpio/BUILD_PIGPIO
  is commented out. The submodule is dead weight as currently wired up.
  Found while fact-checking the README intro paragraph's claim that
  qjs-modules wraps pigpio.

    grep -n '#include' quickjs-gpio.c   # no pigpio.h
    grep -n 'pigpio' CMakeLists.txt     # every hit is commented out

- dom-wrapper-property-access-cost-per-level (lib/dom.js:2117-2119, `Element`
  `children` getter, `Factory.for(this).NodeList.cache(...)`): once the
  parser-depth bug and querySelectorAll's ancestor-check were both fixed,
  document.querySelectorAll('A') on a real ~16MB bookmarks export (22892
  <A> tags, tree depth now correctly bounded at 18) was still slow.
  Benchmarked with perf_hooks.now() over the same 1000 matched paths, four
  ways: applyPath (lib/dom.js:101-122, a plain JS `obj = obj[k]` loop)
  against the live wrapper averaged 9.53ms/call; native Pointer.deref()
  (src/pointer.c:643, pointer_deref) against the same live wrapper
  averaged 11.03ms/call - *slower*, not faster, ruling out "JS loop
  overhead" as the cause. Both against the raw object instead (bypassing
  Node/Element wrapper construction entirely) dropped to 0.018ms/call
  (applyPath) and 0.0014ms/call (deref) - a ~500-1000x difference from the
  same two traversal methods on the same paths, isolating the cost
  entirely to the live wrapper's property access chain, not to
  applyPath's implementation or to using JS vs native C for the walk.
  Each `obj[k]` on a live wrapper goes through Proxy-backed machinery
  (`children` getter above, and/or other Factory/NodeList/Collection
  Proxies) that is expensive enough per call, at every path level, to
  dominate everything else; not yet root-caused which specific step
  (NodeList construction, Factory.cache, or the per-element Proxy in
  Collection/NodeList) is responsible, or whether it's fixable by
  memoizing/caching more aggressively vs. inherent to the Proxy design.

    import { performance } from 'perf_hooks';
    import { Parser } from 'dom';
    const doc = new Parser().parseFromFile('bookmarks.html', 'utf-8'); // real, large, deep-ish document
    const t0 = performance.now();
    const nodes = [...doc.querySelectorAll('A')];
    console.log(performance.now() - t0, nodes.length); // ~9-11ms per match,
                                                         // dominated by wrapper
                                                         // property access

- ctest-nondeterministic-failures: not reproducible on HEAD (2026-11-26).
  40 runs of tests/unittests/test-child-process.js, 40 runs of a lone
  spawnSync(), 6 ctest rounds over child-process, node-child-process,
  pointer, repeater, streams and dom, and a valgrind run of the child
  process test: no crash, no failure, no invalid access. The earlier
  crashes came from a stale build/x86_64-linux-gnu. The repeat-loop tests
  in test-child-process.js will catch a return. Delete this entry if it
  stays quiet.

- misc-dequote-does-not-remove-quotes (quickjs-misc.c:2627-2666):
  `misc.dequote(s)` is not the inverse of `misc.quote(s)`, despite
  doc/native/misc.md describing them as "Add / remove quoting". Both
  `quote` (magic 0) and `dequote` (magic 1) always wrap their output in
  the quote character (`dbuf_putc(&output, quote)` runs unconditionally
  before *and* after, for both magics) - `magic` only switches whether the
  interior bytes get escaped (`dbuf_put_escaped_table`) or unescaped
  (`dbuf_put_unescaped_table`). So `dequote('"a\\"b"')` unescapes the
  backslash but still adds a fresh pair of quote characters around the
  result, producing `"a"b""`-shaped output instead of stripping the
  surrounding quotes. tests/unittests/test-misc.js tests the real
  (always-wraps) behavior rather than the doc's "remove quoting"
  description.

    import * as misc from 'misc';
    misc.dequote(misc.quote('a"b'));   // '""a"b""', not 'a"b'

- misc-objectrefcount-struct-scraping (src/utils.c:1134-1162): same
  struct-scraping fragility as misc-classid-struct-scraping-segfault
  above, on a different pair of functions. `js_object_classid(v)` reads
  `((uint16_t*)p)[3]` and `js_object_refcount(v)` reads `((int*)p)[0]` off
  the object's raw `JSObject*` pointer, assuming a fixed private struct
  layout. On the currently vendored QuickJS these offsets are wrong:
  `misc.objectClassId({})` always returns 0 (a plain object's real class id
  is non-zero) instead of throwing or erroring, and `misc.objectRefCount({})`
  returns a different large, effectively-random int32 on every call -
  looks like it's reading a pointer field (varies with ASLR) rather than
  the refcount. Doesn't segfault (unlike the classId/className/classAtom
  case), just returns silently-wrong data, which is arguably worse since
  nothing signals the failure. tests/unittests/test-misc.js does not
  assert on the values of objectClassId()/objectRefCount(), only that
  they return a number.

    import * as misc from 'misc';
    misc.objectClassId({});   // 0, every time (should be a real class id)
    misc.objectRefCount({});  // a different huge number every run

- lexer-rule-expansion-never-freed (src/lexer.c: lexer_rule_compile ~146,
  lexer_rule_release_rt ~228): lexer_rule_compile() stores a js_strndup()ed
  copy of the expanded pattern in `rule->expansion`, but
  lexer_rule_release_rt() frees only name, expr and bytecode, so one copy
  of every compiled rule's pattern is kept until the runtime is freed.

- lexer-skipbytes-overflows-charpos (quickjs-lexer.c:797-806): after
  consuming a token with `next()`/`nextToken()`, calling
  `lexer.skipBytes(n)` with a valid `n` (1 <= n <= the byte length of the
  token just consumed - the bound `skipBytes()` itself enforces, by
  comparing against `lex->byte_length`, i.e. the *current token's* length,
  not the remaining input) leaves `lexer.charPos` at 4294967296 (2^32) -
  an unsigned 32-bit wraparound, not a small, sensible cursor advance.
  100% reproducible (3/3 runs). Not root-caused past `js_lexer_method`'s
  `LEXER_SKIP_BYTES` case calling `lexer_skip_n(lex, n)`
  (quickjs-lexer.c:803) - didn't trace into `lexer_skip_n` itself (not
  found in a quick grep of src/lexer.c under that exact name). Separately,
  calling `skipBytes(n)` with `n` greater than the current token's byte
  length (including calling it right after construction, before any token
  has been scanned, when the "current token" length is 0) throws
  `InternalError: skipBytes(): count n > <byte_length>` - surprising given
  the doc table lists `skipBytes`/`skipChars` as taking 0 args, but not a
  bug once you know `n` is bounded by the current token, not the input.
  Worked around in tests/unittests/test-lexer.js by only calling
  skipBytes() with no argument (a harmless no-op) rather than exercising
  the overflow.

    import { Lexer } from 'lexer';
    const l = new Lexer('12 foo', Lexer.LONGEST, 'in.txt');
    l.addRule('number', /[0-9]+/);
    l.next();            // consumes "12", current token byte_length becomes 2
    l.skipBytes(1);      // valid (1 <= 2), but...
    l.charPos;            // 1 (2026-11-26; was 4294967296), expected 3

- pgsql-async-connect-live-server-hangs: an async pgsql connect (set
  pq.nonblocking = true, then pq.connect(...)) against a reachable server
  never settles cleanly. with deliberately wrong credentials against a local
  postgres on 127.0.0.1:5432 the promise resolves with undefined (not the
  connection, and not a rejection for the failed login), the then-callback
  fires repeatedly, and the process then hangs instead of exiting. an
  unreachable server now rejects correctly (js_pgconn_connect_cont handles
  PGRES_POLLING_FAILED), so the remaining defect is in the multi-step polling
  path of js_pgconn_connect_cont / js_pgconn_connect_start
  (quickjs-pgsql.c:1030-1125), which is not root-caused. found while checking
  that js_iohandler_fn works for pgsql. not tested with valid credentials.

    import { PGconn } from 'pgsql';
    const pq = new PGconn();
    pq.nonblocking = true;
    await pq.connect('127.0.0.1', 'nosuchuser', 'wrong', 'nosuchdb', 5432, 5);
    // expected: rejection with an authentication error
    // actual: resolves undefined; repeated callbacks; process hangs

- mysql-error-null-after-failed-connect (quickjs-mysql.c): after
  `await db.connect(...)` to a closed port, db.errno is 2002 but db.error
  is null instead of the client library's message, and connect() resolves
  (to the MySQL object) rather than rejecting with a MySQLError. found
  while writing tests/unittests/test-mysql.js.

    import { MySQL } from 'mysql';
    const db = new MySQL();
    await db.connect({ host: '127.0.0.1', port: 1, user: 'x', password: '', db: 'y' });
    // expected: rejects with MySQLError, or db.error is a message string
    // actual: resolves; db.errno === 2002, db.error === null

- relative-link-directory-multiarch (CMakeLists.txt): a relative
  "lib/<multiarch>" ends up in LINK_DIRECTORIES, so every module links with
  -Llib/x86_64-linux-gnu (and qjsm with -L<source dir>/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu),
  and each configure prints one CMP0081 "Relative paths not allowed in
  LINK_DIRECTORIES" dev warning per module target (34 of them). Looks like
  the multiarch libdir (CMAKE_ARCH_LIBDIR) is used where QUICKJS_LIBRARY_DIR
  is meant.

    cmake -S . -B /tmp/b 2>&1 | grep -c CMP0081
    grep -o -- '-Llib/[^ ]*' /tmp/b/CMakeFiles/qjs-bjson.dir/link.txt
    // expected: 0 warnings, no relative -L
    // actual: one warning per module, "-Llib/x86_64-linux-gnu"

- stream-registered-twice (cmake/qjsm.cmake): `stream` is both a native
  module and a JS builtin name once `streams` is pulled in as a dependency;
  the builtin table gets two entries for related names. Observed while
  resolving BUILTINS_NEEDED, not yet checked which one `import 'stream'`
  reaches.

    qjsm -l | grep -i stream

- import-so-suffix-misses-installed (src/qjsm.c): `import 'path.so'` does
  not reach an installed `path.so` when `path` is also builtin; the builtin
  shadows it and there is no spelling for the installed file (see
  doc/qjsm-layout.md 4).

    qjsm -e 'import("path.so").then(m=>print(Object.keys(m).length))'

- lexer-imports-util-by-file-path (lib/lexer/cpp.js:6,
  lib/lexer/ecmascript.js:1): both import '../util.js', a file path, but
  util is a builtin compiled into qjsm and `cmake --install` leaves
  util.js out of lib/quickjs. In an installation without a stale
  util.js (a fresh DESTDIR, a new prefix) lexer/cpp, lexer/ecmascript and
  everything that imports them fail to load: lexer/c, lexer/bnf,
  lexers, c-functions, parser/cpp. /usr/local only works because an old
  install left util.js there. found by utilities/api-surface.sh.

    // QUICKJS_MODULE_PATH=<fresh DESTDIR>/usr/local/lib/quickjs
    import 'lexer/cpp.js';
    // expected: loads (a bare 'util' resolves to the builtin)
    // actual: ReferenceError: could not load module '.../util.js'

- xmlserializer-read-n-splits-utf8 (quickjs-xml.c, js_xmlserializer_read):
  read(n) in string mode cuts the output at n bytes and decodes each piece
  on its own, so a multi-byte character that straddles a cut becomes
  U+FFFD in both pieces; the concatenation differs from read(1000).
  found by tests/tools/xml-write-golden.js.

    const s = new XMLSerializer([{tagName: 'a', children: ['ü€']}]);
    let out = '', c;
    while((c = s.read(1)) !== '') out += c;
    // expected: '<a>ü€</a>'
    // actual: replacement characters in place of ü€

- xml-doctype-internal-subset-mangled (src/xml.c, bang-tag scan): a
  DOCTYPE with an internal subset ends at the first '>' inside the
  subset, so the subset is split into a bogus tag name and a text node
  "]>", and entities declared in it are not available. found while
  writing doc/api-compare-xml.md.

    xml.read('<!DOCTYPE a [<!ENTITY foo "val">]><a>&foo;</a>');
    // expected: a !DOCTYPE node holding the whole declaration
    // actual: [{tagName: '!DOCTYPE a [<!ENTITY foo "val"'}, ']>', {tagName: 'a', ...}]

- xmlpushparser-silent-on-mismatch (quickjs-xml.c, xread_callback):
  XMLPushParser.write() accepts a mismatched end tag without an error and
  the position of a later close() failure is not reported.

    const p = new XMLPushParser(); p.write('<a>\n <b></c></a>'); p.close();
    // expected: SyntaxError at 2:5
    // actual: no error, p.root holds a repaired tree


- ecmascript-lexer-escaped-identifier: lib/lexer/ecmascript.js does not lex
  an identifier with a unicode escape, valid JS since ES2015: a and
  \u{62} give UNEXPECTED_CHARACTER at the backslash. IdentifierStart has
  "\\"[u]{HexDigit}{4}, which the native lexer apparently reads as the
  literal text, and the \u{...} form is missing. module-lexer reports the
  error and goes on. Found 2026-10-20.

    import { parse } from 'lib/module-lexer.js';
    parse('var \\u0061 = 1').error
    // { code: 'UNEXPECTED_CHARACTER', line: 1, column: 5 }, expected none

- resolve-imports-bundle-name-clash: the bundler concatenates files without
  renaming top-level names, so two files that declare the same name give a
  bundle that does not load. Found 2026-10-20 bundling magic-string, whose
  index.mjs and @jridgewell/sourcemap-codec both declare intToChar.

    resolve-imports.js -o b.mjs ../../magic-string/dist/index.mjs
    qjsm b.mjs   # SyntaxError: invalid redefinition of global identifier

- typescript-test-gated-on-swc-cli: test-typescript.js runs only when the
